# G071 — Page Scraper

*v2. See `page-scraper.md` for prose: parse as inverse of render but
partial not total, forgiving parser following Postel, CSS selectors
as DSL, descendant combinator as path-based pattern matching,
termination/memory-bound/no-crash as safety properties.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Summarise an Article

[summarise-article @url
    IN ORDER TO
        fetch a URL, forgivingly parse its HTML
        extract the h1 title and the article paragraphs
        and summarise the body only if it is substantial

    @html <- [http[get {url: @url}]]
    @tree <- [scraper[parse {html: @html}]]

    @title <- [scraper[text-of
                         {node: [scraper[select
                                           {tree: @tree,
                                            selector: "h1"}]] first}]]
    @body  <- [scraper[select
                         {tree: @tree, selector: "article p"}]]
              mapped to [scraper[text-of {node: @p}]]
              joined by "\n\n"
    @links <- [scraper[select
                         {tree: @tree, selector: "article a"}]]
              mapped to @a:attrs:href

    @body
        where length is greater than 200
            @summary <- [llm[summarise {text: @body}]]
            -> {title: @title, summary: @summary, citations: @links}
        otherwise
            -> #content-thin {title: @title}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[scraper
    parse             {html}                   ;; forgiving, never resists
    select            {tree, selector}
    select-recursive  {tree, chain}
    text-of           {node}
    attrs-of          {node}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: forgiving parser `where` malformed becomes best-effort

[scraper[parse {html: @input}]
    IN ORDER TO
        produce a structurally-valid tree from any input
        where the parser never resists
        because real web pages are messy and the alternative is a blank screen

    tokens of @input
        where unknown construct  -> emit as text
        where stray close tag    -> drop
        where unclosed tag at EOF -> close implicitly
        where invalid entity     -> pass through verbatim
    bounded by input length (termination)
    bounded by input size (memory linear in input)
]

Postel's principle as a resolver contract: be strict in what you
send, liberal in what you accept. G069's render is total; G071's
parse is partial, and recovery IS the spec.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: descendant combinator as recursive `where` match

[scraper[select {tree: @t, selector: "article p"}]
    walk @t depth-first
        where node matches "article" (first chain element)
            -> recurse into its descendants looking for "p"
        always also
            -> continue searching @t for more "article" matches
                (nested articles still produce their own p matches)
]

Both rules apply simultaneously. Same algorithm XPath, JSONPath,
filesystem globs, and git ref-matching all use.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @html <- "<article><h1>Hi</h1><p>one</p><p>two</p></article>"
    @tree <- [scraper[parse {html: @html}]]

    -> [scraper[text-of
                  {node: [scraper[select
                                    {tree: @tree,
                                     selector: "h1"}]] first}]]
    ;; -> "Hi"

    -> [scraper[select {tree: @tree, selector: "article p"}]]
       mapped to [scraper[text-of {node: @p}]]
    ;; -> ["one", "two"]
]
